  "rating": {
    "inherent": "critical",
    "residual": "high",
    "forecast": "critical",
    "rationale": "Inherent risk is critical because an untrusted external source (vendor quote email) reaches an S3 sink (irreversible payment delegation) with an autonomy modifier applying (no step or runtime limit). Residual drops to high only because a weak, behavioral, model-dependent prompt rule ('only pay approved-list vendors') provides step-4 credit; no structural control verifies the actual payment destination, no approval gate covers the payment itself (the manager approval occurs before the vendor/bank details are known and is not propagated to payments-agent, per rubric TC7 authority laundering), and payments-agent blindly trusts the orchestrator's bearer token (TC4 confused deputy) with an unsigned, unpinned agent card. A missing spend/rate limit on payment delegation is tracked as a separate property finding. Forecast returns to critical because the one credited control is model-dependent and fails under model_upgrade, longer_task_horizon, or autonomy_increase, at which point the path is effectively uncontrolled."
  },
